## VERDICT: PASS

---

### Executive Summary

Both grounds are closed, and V-1 is closed by inverting the rule rather than by
lengthening the list of blocked values -- which is the difference between a fix
and a patch.

### V-1 closure -- the default is now deny

`READY` is no longer the fall-through. It requires every check to be positively
recognized as bucket `pass`, bucket `skipping`, or state `WAITING`; everything
else blocks, with `UNRECOGNIZED` naming the value the tool did not understand.

The plan makes the argument that matters: failing closed on an unknown value
makes correctness independent of how completely the author enumerated GitHub's
check vocabulary, which is not this repository's to control and will change
without notice. A fall-through rule needs perfect knowledge of that vocabulary
forever; this one needs none. The `cancel` bucket is named as the concrete case
-- a cancelled required check did not pass, is not running, and would have been
waved through.

`test_unrecognized_bucket_blocks` is declared, so the inversion is a behavior and
not a sentence.

### V-2 closure -- revised at iteration 3, after the specified remedy failed

Iteration 2 accepted wiring the invocation into the seal skill and dismissed the
size cost as ordinary. The implement pass proved that wrong, and the correction
is affirmed here rather than papered over.

`qor-substantiate/SKILL.md` stands at 2,702 B of slack against the 2,700 B floor
`test_ladder_rewrite_left_usable_slack` enforces. A 708 B wiring note breached it;
so did the 185 B pointer that replaced it. Every remaining route to those bytes
ran through compressing normative gate text -- the runtime-principal fidelity
directive at line 191, the presence-only seal gate at line 189 -- and the floor's
own docstring records why that is forbidden: three phases each resolved a size
breach under pressure and each made the next one harder.

The edit was reverted, the skill is byte-identical to `main`, and the rule went to
`doctrine-governance-enforcement.md` section 17 -- where merge rules belong, where
Step 9.6 already points, and where no size budget applies. V-2's substance is met:
the module is referenced by a normative document the merge step cites. The seal
skill's need for a progressive-disclosure pass is recorded in the plan as a
finding rather than paid for out of a gate directive.

Two properties of this correction are worth stating. The floor test refused a
change proposed by this tribunal and was obeyed rather than amended, which is the
only reading under which such a floor means anything. And the placement note is in
the plan, so a later reader learns why the wiring is not where they would look for
it.

### Verified by execution (carried forward)

- All three most recent pull requests carry sixteen passing checks and exactly
  one `publish` check in state `WAITING`; the plan's table matches the measured
  payloads and the naive rule would refuse every merge.
- `ac_close_guard.py:119` shells `["gh", *args]` and four further modules do the
  same; `test_no_qor_module_imports_a_forge_sdk` bans SDK imports, not CLI
  invocation, so the declared non-goal is satisfiable as written.
- The event does not age out: the sweep expires unaddressed severity 1-2 after 90
  days and escalates severity 3 and above to severity 5.
- `plan_grep_lint` reports 0 unresolved citations and `dod_check` exits 0.

### On the honesty of the closure

The plan concedes that no control here can be preventive, because `--admin`
exists to bypass exactly the checks a preventive control would install. It closes
a severity-3 event on a detective and procedural instrument and says so in those
words rather than dressing it up. That concession is what makes the closure
defensible: the remedy on the record asks for a rule to be followed, and this
turns the rule from a judgement made at the moment of least patience into a
question one command answers.

